---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import "../styles/global.css";

const jsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Restaurant",
  name: "The Burnham Restaurant",
  servesCuisine: ["American", "Contemporary American", "Comfort Food"],
  priceRange: "$$",
  telephone: "+1-216-413-5120",
  url: "https://www.hilton.com/en/hotels/cledohh-hilton-cleveland-downtown/dining/",
  address: {
    "@type": "PostalAddress",
    streetAddress: "100 Lakeside Ave E",
    addressLocality: "Cleveland",
    addressRegion: "OH",
    postalCode: "44114",
    addressCountry: "US",
  },
  description:
    "Signature restaurant of the Hilton Cleveland Downtown, serving elevated Midwest comfort food for breakfast, lunch, and dinner.",
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="The Burnham Menu Guide (2026) — Prices & What to Order"
  description="An independent guide to The Burnham at the Hilton Cleveland Downtown: Midwest comfort food classics, reported prices, signature dishes like the knife-and-fork po'boy, and visiting tips. Prices reported as of October 2026."
  path="/burnham-menu/"
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
  ogImage="/images/burnham-dining.webp"
>
  <article class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6">
    <img
      src="/images/burnham-dining.webp"
      alt="Illustration of The Burnham's modern dining room with Lake Erie views"
      class="w-full h-64 sm:h-96 object-cover rounded-2xl mt-8"
      fetchpriority="high"
    />

    <p class="mt-8 text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-widest text-gold-dark">
      Menu Guide · Downtown Cleveland, Ohio
    </p>
    <h1 class="font-display text-3xl sm:text-5xl font-bold mt-2 leading-tight">
      The Burnham at Hilton Cleveland Downtown — Menu Guide
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-4 text-lg text-ink-soft leading-relaxed">
      The Burnham is the signature restaurant of the Hilton Cleveland Downtown at 100 Lakeside
      Avenue East — a 257-seat dining room named for Daniel Burnham, the architect whose 1903
      Group Plan shaped Cleveland's civic center. The kitchen's brief is "elevated Midwest
      comfort food": familiar classics rebuilt with modern technique. Cleveland restaurateur
      Zack Bruell consulted on the menus, and the room runs breakfast and lunch daily with
      dinner Monday through Saturday. Below is our independent walkthrough of the menu,
      reported prices, and what we'd order. Everything is written in our own words; prices are
      reported figures, not a copy of the restaurant's menu.
    </p>

    <!-- At a glance -->
    <div class="mt-8 rounded-2xl border border-line bg-white p-6 sm:p-8">
      <h2 class="!mt-0 font-display text-xl font-bold">At a glance</h2>
      <dl class="mt-4 grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2 text-sm">
        <div><dt class="font-semibold text-ink">Location</dt><dd class="text-ink-soft">100 Lakeside Ave E, Cleveland, OH 44114 (inside the Hilton Cleveland Downtown)</dd></div>
        <div><dt class="font-semibold text-ink">Chef</dt><dd class="text-ink-soft">Executive Chef Brandon Park; menus shaped with consultant Zack Bruell</dd></div>
        <div><dt class="font-semibold text-ink">Cuisine</dt><dd class="text-ink-soft">American / Contemporary comfort food</dd></div>
        <div><dt class="font-semibold text-ink">Price level</dt><dd class="text-ink-soft">$31–$50 per person (OpenTable); moderate</dd></div>
        <div><dt class="font-semibold text-ink">Reservations</dt><dd class="text-ink-soft">Recommended, especially during conventions; book via OpenTable or the hotel</dd></div>
        <div><dt class="font-semibold text-ink">Phone</dt><dd class="text-ink-soft">+1 (216) 413-5120</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </div>

    <div class="prose-guide">
      <h2>Breakfast &amp; brunch</h2>
      <p>
        Breakfast is served daily from 6:30am to 11am, and it's a genuine draw — the breakfast
        buffet is popular with both hotel guests and locals, and the Bloody Mary gets special
        attention at brunch. Cleveland Magazine's coverage has flagged the Sunday brunch spread
        (reported around <strong>$32</strong>) as a highlight, with Cleveland touches like
        kielbasa and pierogies alongside themed specials, and an apple pie waffle with apple
        compote and bacon bits (reported <strong>$16</strong>).
      </p>

      <h2>Lunch &amp; dinner</h2>
      <p>
        Lunch runs daily 11:30am–2pm; dinner runs Monday–Saturday 5pm–9pm. The menu reads
        American but travels: reported dishes from published coverage include a knife-and-fork
        po'boy (reported <strong>$16</strong>) built from fried oysters, fried gulf shrimp, and
        coleslaw on a house-made buttermilk biscuit; pork and pineapple fried rice (reported
        <strong>$14</strong>) with Thai basil sauce; duck leg confit (reported <strong>$24</strong>);
        fried cheese curds (reported <strong>$12</strong>); and buttermilk-fried calamari
        (reported <strong>$10</strong>). Crispy-skin salmon, a smash burger, and chicken and
        waffles are the recurring guest favorites.
      </p>
      <p>
        Our take: the po'boy is the signature lunch order — it's the dish the room is known for,
        and it shows the kitchen's comfort-food thesis at its best.
      </p>

      <h2>Drinks &amp; the patio</h2>
      <p>
        The 60-seat patio overlooking Lake Erie and Mall B is one of downtown's better outdoor
        seats. Elsewhere in the hotel, Bar 32 — the 32nd-floor rooftop bar — does the skyline
        views and craft cocktails (21+), while Eliot's Bar on the second floor covers all-day
        drinks and bites.
      </p>

      <h2>What we'd order</h2>
      <p>If we had one meal here, this is how we'd play it:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Lunch:</strong> the knife-and-fork po'boy — decadent, messy, and exactly the point.</li>
        <li><strong>Dinner:</strong> the duck leg confit for something refined, or the smash burger for the comfort-food purist.</li>
        <li><strong>Brunch:</strong> the Sunday spread with a Bloody Mary, especially if you can score a patio table.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Planning your visit</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Watch the convention calendar.</strong> The hotel sits next to the convention center; during big events the dining room fills fast.</li>
        <li><strong>No dinner on Sundays</strong> — plan brunch or look elsewhere for Sunday night.</li>
        <li><strong>Parking:</strong> $15 valet with a dinner purchase; the free RTA B-Line and C-Line trolleys stop nearby.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <!-- Price disclaimer -->
    <div class="mt-10 rounded-xl border border-gold/40 bg-gold/10 p-5 text-sm text-ink-soft leading-relaxed">
      <p class="font-semibold text-ink mb-1">About these prices</p>
      <p>
        Prices above are <strong>reported as of October 2026</strong>, compiled from Cleveland
        Magazine's published dining coverage and the restaurant's official listings. Individual
        dish prices come from that published coverage and may have moved since — always verify
        current prices with the restaurant before visiting.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p class="mt-8 text-sm text-ink-soft">
      <a href="/best-hotel-restaurants-ohio/" class="text-gold-dark font-semibold hover:underline">← Back to Ohio hotel restaurants</a>
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
